otin E^2_{SU}$, $\{j, u_2\} 
otin E^2_{SU}$, and $\{i, u_2\} \in E^2_{SU}$ and $\{j, u_1\} \in E^2_{SU}$. Clearly $(G^2_{SU}, \mathbf{Z}^2_{SU}) \in \mathrm{C}(G_R, \mathbf{d}_R, \mathbf{Z}_R)$." [Counterexample, from the validator] Observed data: $V_R = \{1, 2, 3\}$, $E_R = \{(1, 2), (2, 3)\}$, $M = \{1\}$, $(t_1, t_2, t_3) = (1, 2, 3)$, $\mathbf{d}_R = (3, 2, 3)$, $\mathbf{Z}_R = (0, 0, 1)$; so $(d_1^r, d_2^r, d_3^r) = (1, 2, 1)$. Take $i = 1$, $j = 3$: $d_1 > d_1^r$, $d_3 > d_3^r$, $Z_1 = 0 
eq 1 = Z_3$, so the hypotheses of Result 2 hold, with the normalization $Z_i = 0$, $Z_j = 1$ of the proof. Let $V^1_{SU} = \{1, 2, 3, 4, 5\}$ with $u_1 = 4$, $u_2 = 5$ unsampled, $Z^1_4 = 0$, $Z^1_5 = 1$, and $E^1_{SU} = \{\{1, 2\}, \{2, 3\}, \{1, 4\}, \{3, 5\}, \{1, 5\}, \{3, 4\}\}$. The degrees are $(3, 2, 3, 2, 2)$; the pair satisfies conditions 1–4 of Definition 3 and contains $\{i, u_1\} = \{1, 4\}$ and $\{j, u_2\} = \{3, 5\}$: it is one of the starting pairs the proof allows. It also contains the cross edges $\{i, u_2\} = \{1, 5\}$ and $\{j, u_1\} = \{3, 4\}$. The pair described as $(G^2_{SU}, \mathbf{Z}^2_{SU})$ ($\{1, 4\}$ and $\{3, 5\}$ absent, $\{1, 5\}$ and $\{3, 4\}$ present, all else the same) has $E^2_{SU} = \{\{1, 2\}, \{2, 3\}, \{1, 5\}, \{3, 4\}\}$ and degrees $(2, 2, 2, 1, 1)$: the sampled degrees are not $(3, 2, 3)$, condition 4 of Definition 3 fails, and $(G^2_{SU}, \mathbf{Z}^2_{SU}) 
otin \mathrm{C}(G_R, \mathbf{d}_R, \mathbf{Z}_R)$, contrary to the printed "Clearly". The degrees and $|E_{SU}|$ differ between the two pairs, so the common denominator used in (A1) is not available either.

