otin B$ (a recruitment edge runs from the earlier to the later vertex, and $(i, j) 
otin E_R$ with $i < j$). Let $G^1_{SU}$ be the graph whose vertices are those of $V_R$, one new unsampled vertex $u$, and further new unsampled vertices, $s_i - 1$ of them joined only to $i$, $s_j - 1$ joined only to $j$, and $s_v$ joined only to $v$ for every other $v \in V_R$; its edges are those of $B$, the edges $\{i, u\}$ and $\{j, u\}$, and the edges just described. Then: (a) $G^1_{SU}$ satisfies conditions 1, 3 and 4 of Definition 3 (with any binary traits on its unsampled vertices, $(G^1_{SU}, \mathbf{Z}^1_{SU}) \in \mathrm{C}(G_R, \mathbf{d}_R, \mathbf{Z}_R)$), it contains $\{i, u\}$ and $\{j, u\}$, and $\{i, j\} 
otin E^1_{SU}$. Let $G^2_{SU}$ be obtained from $G^1_{SU}$ by removing the edges $\{i, u\}$, $\{j, u\}$ and the vertex $u$, and adding the edge $\{i, j\}$. Then: (b) $G^2_{SU}$ satisfies conditions 1, 3 and 4 of Definition 3, and $G^2_{SU} 
eq G^1_{SU}$, also up to renaming unsampled vertices ($\{i, j\}$ is an edge of $G^2_{SU}$ only). Because there exist at least 2 augmented recruitment-induced subgraphs compatible with the observed data, $G_{SU}$ is not identified. (c) Let $G^1_S$, $G^2_S$ be the recruitment-induced subgraphs obtained by removing the unsampled vertices (and the edges connected to them) from $G^1_{SU}$, $G^2_{SU}$. Their edge sets are $B$ and $B \cup \{\{i, j\}\}$. Both are compatible with the observed data in the sense defined at the opening of this proof, and $\{i, j\} 
otin G^1_S$, $\{i, j\} \in G^2_S$. Because there exist at least 2 recruitment-induced subgraphs compatible with the observed data, $G_S$ is not identified.
